    <div class="card stop">
      <span class="tag">Blocker 02 · verified</span>
      <h3>No FEA toolchain exists here</h3>
      <p><code>ccx</code>, <code>gmsh</code>, <code>meshio</code>, <code>pyvista</code>, <code>cma</code>, <code>fastapi</code> — none installed. Yet the plan quotes precise cantilever results (238.9 → 240.7 → 244.4 MPa, 2.7935 mm). Either a teammate has a working solver elsewhere, or those numbers are aspirational. <strong>This one answer decides whether Phase 1 is 3 hours or 12.</strong></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card stop">
      <span class="tag">Blocker 03 · verified</span>
      <h3>Two repos that never met</h3>
      <p><code>main</code> holds the planning docs. <code>setup/skeleton</code> holds every line of working code. Their only shared commit is the initial <code>9396d28</code>. Merge is a ten-minute job that currently blocks all four lanes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What we are already doing well</h2>
  <p>Worth stating plainly, because the plan is written as though the repo were empty. Four of these are <em>stronger</em> than what the plan asks for.</p>

  <ul class="plain">
    <li><strong>The Devin round-trip is proven, not planned.</strong> Plan task 4.1 says “test with one trivial session before wiring the real loop.” Already done: Devin cloned, installed CadQuery 2.8.0 on Python 3.12.10, ran our validator, reproduced our numbers exactly, committed and pushed. And <code>guard.py</code> was run against that real commit and correctly reported that only the permitted file moved.</li>
    <li><strong>The opening failure is designed in, not hoped for.</strong> The baseline plate fails exactly one check for a physically real reason — 6.17 mm of mid-span standoff against a 1.5 mm limit, caused by a 22 mm anterior bow — while 12 other checks pass. The plan’s archetype v1 starts already contoured, so it has no guaranteed first-frame failure. Ours is the better demo.</li>
    <li><strong>“Not just an optimiser” is mechanically enforced.</strong> Plan task 3.4 <em>hopes</em> the generator stays structurally extensible. Ours makes parameter-only fixes impossible: set <code>ribs=[…]</code> without writing the geometry and <code>build_implant()</code> raises <code>NotImplementedError</code>. That is a stronger answer to the sharpest jury question than the plan has.</li>
    <li><strong>The design space is calibrated infeasible, with the arithmetic written down.</strong> <code>design_space_note</code> in <code>case.json</code> shows uniform thickening buys ~5% volume against the 39 g budget (≈1.11× stress reduction — not enough), widening hits a keepout at 17 mm, and lengthening hits keepouts at both ends. The plan gestures at anti-gaming via mass and stiffness bounds; we did the sums.</li>
    <li><strong>Every check already returns structure, never a bare boolean</strong> — <code>{status, value, limit, unit, location, message}</code> — which is exactly the shape plan task 1.4 specifies, and it is additive-only so it can absorb the plan’s <code>tag</code> field without breaking anyone.</li>
