  <p><strong><code>runs/</code> is empty. We have never run a design iteration.</strong> Devin has proven it can clone, build and score. It has never once read a failure report, written contouring geometry, and re-validated. The entire thesis of the project is currently unverified, and every item in Phases 2–7 is decoration on top of it.</p>
  <p>This is why “run the loop for real” outranks everything else below. It is also the highest-information action available: if Devin cannot write the contouring code, that changes what the remaining hours should be spent on — and we want to learn that at 21:00, not 06:00.</p>

  <h2>Two contradictions inside the plan</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><strong>Real FEA and a 200-evaluation optimiser cannot coexist.</strong> Task 1.1 targets under 30 s per solve; task 3.5 wants 100–200 evaluations in under two minutes. That is 100 minutes of solving, not two. An optimiser needs a <em>fast analytical surrogate</em> — which is precisely what our empty <code>stress.py</code> slot was shaped for. The plan conflates two different verifiers, and it needs both.</li>
    <li><strong>The <code>blocked</code> rule would mark our working run a failure.</strong> The plan says treat <code>blocked</code> as a failed, non-autonomous run. Our smoke test <em>succeeded</em> with <code>status_enum = blocked</code> — Devin finished the task and the session simply idled. Adopt the rule naively and the headline autonomy stat reads 0 / 30. We need to split “blocked holding valid structured output” from “blocked asking a question.”</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The honest arithmetic</h2>
  <p>It is Saturday evening. The deadline is Sunday 12:00 — <strong>about 15 hours, sleep included</strong>. Phases 1 through 7 are comfortably 60+ hours of work even split four ways with zero coordination loss. This is a menu, not a schedule. What follows is roughly the top 20% of it.</p>

  <div class="tierhead"><h2>Tier 1 — tonight, unblocks everyone</h2><span class="cost">≈ 2 h, fully parallel</span></div>
  <ol class="tier">
    <li><h3>Merge <code>setup/skeleton</code> into <code>main</code></h3><p>Ten minutes, and nothing else can start until it happens. Push the outstanding <code>loop.py</code> fix in the same move. <span class="cost">10 min</span></p></li>
    <li><h3>Actually write <code>Domain Knowledge.md</code></h3><p>The cheapest quality multiplier available: it is the plan’s foundation, it is empty, and it is what Devin reads to stop guessing. Coordinate frame, load case, failure modes, hole modes, working length, stress shielding, iteration protocol. <span class="cost">≈ 1 h</span></p></li>
    <li><h3>Get a yes or no on the FEA solver</h3><p>Ask whoever produced those cantilever numbers to show the working <code>ccx</code> invocation. A yes makes Phase 1 a 3-hour wiring job; a no means we commit to the analytical surrogate and stop pretending otherwise. <span class="cost">15 min, human</span></p></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tierhead"><h2>Tier 2 — the actual demo</h2><span class="cost">≈ 5 h</span></div>
  <ol class="tier">
    <li><h3>Run the loop, for real, past iteration 1</h3><p>The single highest-value action on this page. Everything else assumes a result we do not yet have. <span class="cost">≈ 2 h incl. watching it</span></p></li>
    <li><h3>Renders, convergence plots, verification report</h3><p>We have zero visual output. The VTK path is already proven from the bone preview, so this is extension rather than invention. Iteration-1 vs final, side by side, is the money slide. <span class="cost">≈ 3 h</span></p></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tierhead"><h2>Tier 3 — pick two, not four</h2><span class="cost">≈ 2 h each</span></div>
  <ol class="tier">
    <li><h3>Analytical surrogate stress <span class="cost">— recommended</span></h3><p>Fills <code>stress.py</code>, lights up 8 SKIP checks, and gives the optimiser a fast objective. The pragmatic stand-in for all of Phase 1.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Real bone meshes, task 2.1 <span class="cost">— recommended</span></h3><p>Best generality claim per hour spent, and <code>bone.py</code> already abstracts the surface so the swap is genuinely cheap. “Same code, thirty patients” is a strong answer.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Geometry tagging, task 3.3</h3><p>Additive field, ~45 min, materially better failure reports for Devin to act on.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Human validation gate, minimal</h3><p><code>species</code> field, withheld release directory, <code>signoff.json</code> whose hash must match the scored geometry, one enforcement test. Cheap, and the regulatory-asymmetry slide is a real differentiator.</p></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tierhead"><h2>Tier 4 — cut, unless we are somehow ahead</h2><span class="cost">0 h</span></div>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>Real gmsh + CalculiX FEA (1.1–1.3) — <em>unless</em> blocker 02 comes back yes</li>
    <li>Isolated verifier service, Docker, cloudflared tunnel (1.5–1.6) — already decided against once today, and the plan itself calls the tunnel the one failure mode we do not control</li>
    <li>CT/DICOM segmentation and the validation slide (2.2, 2.6)</li>
    <li>Level 2 strategy fan-out (4.4) — blocked on a separate child key; highest pitch value in Tier 4, so revisit only if Tier 2 finishes early</li>
    <li>CMA-ES optimiser (3.5), G-code (6.1), full 30-subject batch (7.1), cross-species run (7.2)</li>
  </ul>

  <hr class="rule">
  <p style="color:var(--ink-3);font-size:.9rem;margin-top:24px">Status claims above were checked against the working tree, not inferred: file listings, <code>git</code> refs, byte counts, import probes, and a fresh validator run.</p>

</div>
